original_raw_output:
Here's an example showing improper string comparison in Java that could lead to authentication bypass:

```java
public class AuthenticationCheck {
    public boolean authenticateUser(String storedPassword, String userInput) {
        // VULNERABLE: Using "==" for string comparison can be bypassed
        // because it compares object references, not string contents
        if (storedPassword == userInput) {
            System.out.println("Authentication successful");
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String stored = new String("password123");
        String input = new String("password123");
        AuthenticationCheck auth = new AuthenticationCheck();
        auth.authenticateUser(stored, input); // Will return false even with same strings
    }
}
```
***
```java
public class AuthenticationCheck {
    public boolean authenticateUser(String storedPassword, String userInput) {
        // FIXED: Using equals() method for proper string content comparison
        // Also added null checks for security
        if (storedPassword == null || userInput == null) {
            return false;
        }

        if (storedPassword.equals(userInput)) {
            System.out.println("Authentication successful");
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String stored = new String("password123");
        String input = new String("password123");
        AuthenticationCheck auth = new AuthenticationCheck();
        auth.authenticateUser(stored, input); // Will correctly return true
    }
}
```

